    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 162,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54695,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 68,
        "headingId": 162,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Acceptable:</em> cloacogenic polyp.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-03-04T10:22:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 363,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 163,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54696,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 68,
        "headingId": 163,
        "headingText": "<p>None</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-03-04T10:22:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 364,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54697,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 68,
        "headingId": 164,
        "headingText": "<p>ICP is located in the anal transitional zone, but it may involve the lower rectum, often with origin from the anterior wall of the region.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-03-04T10:22:41"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 365,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54698,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 68,
        "headingId": 165,
        "headingText": "<p>The common clinical presentation of ICP includes rectal bleeding, constipation, swelling of the anus, and pruritus. ICP is often detected by endoscopic examination performed for other reasons. Haemorrhoids and diverticulosis may be seen in patients with ICP.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-03-04T10:22:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 366,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54699,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 68,
        "headingId": 166,
        "headingText": "<p>ICP is uncommon and represents &lt;&nbsp;1% of lower GI tract polyps. It is predominantly reported in North American and European populations. There is no sex predilection. The polyp appears to have bimodal distribution, with peak occurrence in the second decade and the seventh decade of life {23932634; 9042143; 8372025}, although it has also been reported in a 6-year-old girl {7595840} and an 85-year-old woman {3500106}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-03-04T10:22:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 367,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54700,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 68,
        "headingId": 167,
        "headingText": "<p>ICP may be related to a low-fibre diet with a resultant increase in colonic intraluminal pressure.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-03-04T10:23:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 368,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54701,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 68,
        "headingId": 168,
        "headingText": "<p>The most accepted pathogenesis of ICP is the intraluminal pressure or abnormal pressure gradients leading to mucosal prolapse, hypoperfusion with ischaemic injury, necrosis, and regeneration.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-03-04T10:23:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 375,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 207,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54702,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 68,
        "headingId": 207,
        "headingText": "<p>ICP is usually small, ranging from 5 to 50&nbsp;mm in greatest dimension (mean: ~20&nbsp;mm) {3500106}. It may be solitary or multiple. The surface of the polyp is often erythematous, oedematous, and eroded.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-03-04T10:23:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 369,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54703,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 68,
        "headingId": 169,
        "headingText": "<p>ICP is covered by stratified squamous epithelium, columnar epithelium, and transitional epithelium (intermediate between squamous and columnar epithelium). The epithelium shows hyperplastic changes with complex tubulovillous growth and goblet cell hypertrophy, with or without ulceration and regenerative nuclear atypia. There may be displacement of dilated (cystic) mucosal glands and pools of mucin in the lamina propria, resembling proctitis cystica profunda. Active chronic inflammation, granulation tissue, vascular ectasia, and oedema are often present. A classic feature is fibromuscular proliferation and obliteration of the lamina propria in a radial fashion. Reactive atypia in the ICP can be mistaken for dysplasia. p53 and Ki-67 immunohistochemistry may be useful in this distinction {18484980}.</p>\r\n<p>ICP is part of the spectrum of diseases with similar pathology and etiology referred to as mucosal prolapse syndrome, which includes solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, rectal prolapse, and proctitis cystica profunda.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-03-04T10:24:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 370,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 170,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54704,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 68,
        "headingId": 170,
        "headingText": "<p>Not clinically relevant</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-03-04T10:24:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 371,
